65: }} With Gemini IV, NASA changed to Roman numerals for Gemini mission designations.</ref> was a 1966 crewed spaceflight in [[NASA]]'s [[Project Gemini|Gemini program]]. It was the seventh crewed [[Project Gemini|Gemini]] flight, the 15th crewed American flight and the 23rd spaceflight of all time (includes [[North American X-15|X-15]] flights over {{convert|100|km|mi nmi|sp=us}}). The original crew for Gemini 9, command pilot [[Elliot See]] and pilot [[Charles Bassett]], were killed in a [[1966 NASA T-38 crash|crash]] on February 28, 1966, while flying a [[Northrop T-38 Talon|T-38 jet trainer]] to the [[McDonnell Aircraft]] plant in [[St. Louis, Missouri]] to inspect their spacecraft. Their deaths promoted the backup crew, [[Thomas P. Stafford]] and [[Gene Cernan|Eugene Cernan]], to the prime crew. The mission was renamed Gemini 9A after the original May 17 launch was scrubbed when the mission's [[Agena target vehicle|Agena Target Vehicle]] was destroyed after a launch failure. The mission was flown June 3–6, 1966, after launch of the backup [[Agena target vehicle|Augmented Target Docking Adaptor]] (ATDA). Stafford and Cernan [[space rendezvous|rendezvous]]ed with the ATDA, but were unable to [[docking and berthing of spacecraft|dock]] with it because the nose fairing had failed to eject from the docking target due to a launch preparation error. Cernan performed a two-hour [[extravehicular activity]], during which it was planned for him to demonstrate free flight in a self-contained rocket pack, the [[United States Air Force|USAF]] Astronaut Maneuvering Unit. He was unable to accomplish this due to stress, fatigue, and overheating.
82:On February 28, 1966, See and Bassett were flying from Texas to inspect the Gemini 9 spacecraft at the [[McDonnell Aircraft Corporation|McDonnell Aircraft]] plant in [[St. Louis]], [[Missouri]]. The conditions at [[St. Louis Lambert International Airport|Lambert Field]] were poor and, as a consequence, in attempting a visual approach and landing, See hit one of the assembly buildings of the factory and caused the aircraft to crash, killing himself and Bassett instantly. As a consequence, the backup crew was promoted to prime crew, the first time this had occurred since the flight of [[Mercury-Atlas 7]] in 1962.<ref name=Titans>{{cite book|last1=Hacker|first1=Barton C.  |last2=Grimwood  |first2=James M.  |title=On the Shoulders of Titans: A History of Project Gemini |chapter=Chapter 14 Charting New Space Lanes  |chapter-url=https://www.hq.nasa.gov/office/pao/History/SP-4203/ch14-2.htm  |publisher=NASA  |series=NASA History Series  |volume=SP-4203  |date=September 1974  |url=http://www.hq.nasa.gov/office/pao/History/SP-4203/toc.htm  |url-status=live  |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100113132344/http://www.hq.nasa.gov/office/pao/History/SP-4203/toc.htm  |archive-date=January 13, 2010
